 ### QEM-based Relocation in Normal Direction
-A vertex relocation method using Quadric Error Metrics (QEM) to improve approximation quality in 3D, replacing simple Lloyd relaxation which moves vertices to Voronoi cell barycenters [S3]. It computes the QEM Matrix of each triangle surrounding the considered vertex, and relocates the vertex according to the sum of these matrices [S3]. For the computation of one QEM matrix, the position is the triangle barycenter projected on the surface (using Newton's algorithm), and the normal direction is the gradient of $f$ at this position [S3]. It detects whether a vertex is located on a feature by analyzing the corresponding QEM matrix $M_p$ using Singular Value Decomposition (SVD). Eigenvalues are sorted by increasing order of magnitude, and any singular value smaller than one twentieth of the largest singular value is discarded [S3]. One significant eigenvalue indicates a planar region, two indicate a sharp edge, and three indicate a corner [S3]. When two or three eigenvalues are used, the vertex is displaced not only along the normal direction but also along the tangent plane [S3].
+A vertex relocation method using Quadric Error Metrics (QEM) to improve approximation quality in 3D, replacing simple Lloyd relaxation which moves vertices to Voronoi cell barycenters [S3]. It computes the QEM Matrix of each triangle surrounding the considered vertex, and relocates the vertex according to the sum of these matrices [S3]. For the computation of one QEM matrix, the position is the triangle barycenter projected on the surface (using Newton's algorithm), and the normal direction is the gradient of $f$ at this position [S3]. It detects whether a vertex is located on a feature by analyzing the corresponding QEM matrix $M_p$ using Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) [S3]. Eigenvalues are sorted by increasing order of magnitude, and any singular value smaller than one twentieth of the largest singular value is discarded [S3]. One significant eigenvalue indicates a planar region, two indicate a sharp edge, and three indicate a corner [S3]. When two or three eigenvalues are used, the vertex is displaced not only along the normal direction but also along the tangent plane [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Solving the $3 \times 3$ linear system is constant time per vertex. SVD adds overhead but is bounded.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for $A$ ($3 \times 3$ matrix) and $\mathbf{b}$ (3D vector) per vertex.
